well brian several national civil rights  attorneys are shedding light on the  death of byron williams  he's an unarmed black man who died in  police custody nearly two years ago  but now his family is suing metro police  this is a story the i-team has been  following in vanessa murphy here now  with more on the latest developments  and also what williams family is  demanding vanessa denise this is a case  which attorneys say  is similar to the murder of george floyd  but for the most part it's gone  under the radar nationally now some big  names in the civil rights community are  stepping in  they're calling out the metro police  department and the clark county district  attorney  byron williams told officers he couldn't  breathe  24 times stevie wonder could see  that this man needed medical help on  thursday byron  life matters civil rights attorneys  including ben crump  the family of byron williams and the  nephew and brother of george floyd  demanded transparency and accountability  you have no choice but to think about  george floyd because the situations are  similar in so many ways  byron williams should not  have been killed by the police  for riding a bicycle while black on  september 5th  2019 metro officers tried stopping  williams for not having a bicycle light  he ran an officer appeared to put a knee  on his back  while williams repeatedly told officers  he couldn't breathe  they did not immediately provide aid or  call for medical attention  they joked williams may just not want to  go to jail  officers turned their body cameras off  and then back on while at the scene  williams was pronounced dead that  morning the clark county coroner ruled  his death a homicide because the prone  restraint officers kept him in was a  factor  and also noted he had methamphetamine in  his system and other health issues  now the family of williams is suing four  officers at the scene  the metro police department sheriff joe  lombardo clark county and the city of  las vegas  according to a metro spokesman all four  officers named in the lawsuit are  currently working  the clark county district attorney  previously announced they would not be  charged i can't believe that they're  still  wearing that uniform that they still  have a badge  and they're supposed to protect and  serve the community  lawyers are also calling for an  investigation by the department of  justice and the nevada attorney general  we reached out to aaron ford's office  and a spokeswoman says they cannot  confirm  or deny whether there is an ongoing  criminal  investigation the legal team for byron  williams family  also claims that metro is not releasing  all of the footage from the scene a  department spokesman tells me that  isn't true and all footage has been  released  now back in october metro announced  policy changes  following william's death pertaining to  use of force  and body cameras as we mentioned this is  a story the i-team has been following  closely  you can watch our reports at  eightnewsnow.com
 

